# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files (can opt-in for committing if needed)
.env*

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# Design reference screenshots (not part of the app)
reference/

# Category-audit thumbnails: 30 MB of derived files, rebuilt in a minute with
# `node scripts/audit-thumbnails.mjs`. The audit's inputs and verdicts under
# audit/batches and audit/out ARE committed — those cannot be regenerated.
audit/thumbs/

# The pre-Postgres file store. Users, messages and the signing secret all live
# in the database now and data/ no longer exists, but the entry stays so an old
# checkout or a script writing there can never commit customer data.
#
# Anchored to the root, and it has to be: unanchored, `data/` matches at any
# depth, so it also swallowed src/data/ — where the catalogue JSON lives. The
# files already tracked there were unaffected and the trap only sprang on the
# next new one, which is the kind that is found late.
/data/

# The old catalogue site's product photos: 390 MB of remove.bg cut-outs that
# scripts/fetch-katalog-images.mjs downloads and scripts/cutout-images.mjs reads
# as an alpha source. Re-fetched in minutes; the cuts made from them under
# public/products ARE committed.
#
# sources/segmented/recipe.json is the exception, and has to be: 2 KB of crops
# read off a grid by eye and decisions made one product at a time, with no second
# copy anywhere and nothing to derive it from — 332 MB of model weights cannot
# reconstruct a judgement. A negation cannot reach into an excluded directory, so
# the parents are opened one at a time to let that one file back through.
sources/*
!sources/segmented/
sources/segmented/*
!sources/segmented/recipe.json
